        "text": "{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"reasons\":[\"Classification is defensible: Article VII gives the Issuer no cause-free exit (removal only on a Servicer Termination Event, and then exercisable by the Indenture Trustee at the Controlling Class's direction), and Section 8.3 runs the Agreement until the last Receivable is paid, so the engagement plainly exceeds 12 months with no customer convenience right — exactly the 'missing behaviour' deviation contemplated by the detection guidance.\",\"Vendor side correctly assessed as better than preferred (Section 7.1 no-resignation plus Section 7.3 continued performance until a Successor Servicer accepts), and the drafter did not over-flag it; the Section 8.1/8.2 Optional Acquisition/Redemption 10-day notice is reasonably explained as not a servicer walk-away, avoiding a double flag.\",\"Redline is minimal and additive: a single new Section 7.1A granting a 30 days' notice convenience termination with no fee/penalty and a refund of prepaid Servicing Fees, expressly routed through the existing Sections 7.3–7.5 successor machinery so no existing operative text is disturbed. It tracks the model language and the preferred position.\",\"The failed deterministic check ('Customer convenience termination present') is a phrasing variant only — the right is conferred on the Issuer/Depositor, which are our-side parties in this document, and the inserted text plainly grants for-convenience termination on 30 days' notice.\",\"Comment is accurate, concise, in the required voice, explains the change and cites the fallback (60–90 days' notice) without lecturing.\",\"Caveat (non-fatal): in this ABS structure the Issuer is an SPE and the Depositor a Verizon affiliate, and removal rights are deliberately vested in the Indenture Trustee for noteholder protection, so the practical leverage of this ask is limited and counterparty/rating-agency pushback should be expected; severity is better treated as medium.\"],\"severityAdjustment\":\"medium\"}"
